    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-q12a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that for any two integers $m$ and $n$, the sum $m+n$ and the difference $m-n$ are either both even or both odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$m+n=(m-n)+2n$, so the sum and difference have the same parity.",
          "worked": "$$m+n=(m-n)+2n.$$\nSince $2n$ is even, adding it does not change parity. Therefore $m+n$ and $m-n$ are either both even or both odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q12b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Let $c$ be an odd integer. Prove that there are no integers $m$ and $n$ for which $m^2-n^2=2c$. Hint: Use part (a) and a proof by contradiction.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No such integers exist.\n\nThe factors $m-n$ and $m+n$ have the same parity, so their product is either odd or divisible by $4$; $2c$ is neither.",
          "worked": "Suppose such integers exist. Factorising gives\n$$\\begin{align*}m^2-n^2&=2c\\\\(m-n)(m+n)&=2c.\\end{align*}$$\nBy part (a), $m-n$ and $m+n$ have the same parity. They cannot both be odd, since their product would be odd but $2c$ is even. Thus they are both even, so their product is divisible by $4$.\n\nHowever, $c$ is odd, so $2c$ is not divisible by $4$. This is a contradiction. Therefore no such integers exist.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      }
    ]
  },
